claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 16:12:02 -0700, Pat Maddox wrote:
>> Several major open source projects use a mailing list to distribute
>> patches. I know some of them have different policies regarding how to
>> post new versions - some post new versions as a reply to the previous
>> version, whereas other post new threads and link to the previous
>> version.
>>
>> What are the specific policies of each project, and why? Also want
>> documentation and examples.

[...]

> Reply in the old thread
> -----------------------
>
> Git. Documentation/SubmittingPatches [i]:
>
> "All subsequent versions of a patch series and other related
> patches should be grouped into their own e-mail thread to help
> readers find all parts of the series. To that end, send them as
> replies to either an additional 'cover letter' message..."
>
> MyFirstContribution says to pass --in-reply-to and "always use the
> Message-ID from v1" [j], and builds v2 with format-patch -v2
> --range-diff. git.git pins this in its .b4-config with
> "send-same-thread = shallow" [k], overriding b4's default. The
> rationale is that readers can find every version in one place.
> Example: "[PATCH v2 0/4] object-file: fix '.keep' lockfile
> ownership...", In-Reply-To the v1 cover [l]. In practice people
> sometimes reply to the previous version instead of v1, so chains
> still deepen.

[...]

> Where this lab stands: we already follow the kernel.
> doc/mail-workflow.md says a revision is "a fresh thread subject
> `[PATCH vN]`, with a `v(N-1): <message-id>` link in the cover", and
> src/lab-process-mail/prompt.md requires the "Link to v(N-1):" line.
> format-patch.sh refuses a reroll without --previous, which also makes
> it attach an interdiff. The superseded tag in "[PATCH v3] notmuch:
> Tag series a later version replaces" [1] reads those Link lines, so
> we get patchwork-style supersession without the reply. That covers
> the one advantage Buildroot cites for replying. Your later decision
> to drop the Overcome-by reply, in "Re: [PATCH 0/3] notmuch: Tag
> superseded series and hide them" [2], means we also skip GCC's
> follow-up in the old thread.
>
> I see no reason to change. If you want shallow reply threading like
> git.git instead, it is a small change to format-patch.sh
> (--in-reply-to=<v1 cover>) plus the doc and prompt; say so and I will
> send it. If you want this survey kept in the tree, I can post it as
> a zib note.

I'm convinced by the git approach. It will be a lot easier for me to
track work going on in the list if it's all in a thread.

Yep, get to work on whatever changes need to be made to support
this.
